<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Engelberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1124 CE – 1798 CE (approx.) · capital Engelberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Benedictine abbey in a high valley of central Switzerland that ruled the valley as a tiny state until 1798 and still flourishes today.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-sellenburen">Conrad of Sellenbüren</a> founded the Benedictine abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a> in 1120 in a remote valley below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titlis">Mount Titlis</a>, with monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muri-abbey">Muri</a>. In 1124 Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-ii">Calixtus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry V</a> confirmed its foundation and imperial protection, making it a free imperial abbey.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbots ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a> valley as lords, with rights of justice over its farmers. Under Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frowin-of-engelberg">Frowin</a> in the 12th century the abbey had a notable scriptorium. Engelberg was protected by the Swiss cantons of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Lucerne</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Schwyz</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Unterwalden</a> and Uri, and dairying and cheese provided its income.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French invasion in 1798 ended the abbey's secular rule, and the valley was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. In 1815 it joined the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obwalden">Obwalden</a>. The monastery survives as an active Benedictine community, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a> is now a resort.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-engelberg.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Engelberg" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Ketty &amp; Alexandre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Engelberg_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1124 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Engelberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frowin of Engelberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Engelberg Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1461&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-engelberg" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Engelberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4878 533 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Engelberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5198 789L5186 782M5198 789L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5198 789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5198 789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1461&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-engelberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
